'''''Reyes v. Ministry of Health''''' (2013–2014) was a landmark {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which [[Adriana Reyes i Soler]], a {{w|transgender}} woman from [[Sant Llàtzer]], challenged the denial of access to {{w|gender-affirming healthcare}} through the national health system. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled in Reyes's favour, finding that the linkage between healthcare access and a medicalised legal recognition process created a discriminatory barrier. The ruling became the primary legal basis for the Gender Recognition Act of 2017, which adopted a {{w|self-determination}} model for legal gender change.

== Background ==

In 2011, the Ministry of Health issued a directive extending {{w|national health service}} coverage to include {{w|hormone replacement therapy}} (HRT) and related bloodwork monitoring for adults who had received a diagnosis of {{w|gender dysphoria}} from a registered psychiatrist. The directive represented the first time that transition-related care was recognised as a covered service under the Balboan National Health Service.

However, access to gender-affirming healthcare was linked to the legal gender recognition process. In order to receive gender-affirming healthcare through the national health system, patients were required to present a court-ordered gender recognition certificate, which in turn required medical and psychological evaluation. This created a circular dependency: a medical diagnosis was required for legal recognition, and legal recognition was required for healthcare access.

[[Adriana Reyes i Soler]] was a transgender woman living in [[Sant Llàtzer]] who sought hormone replacement therapy through her local public hospital in 2013. She was told that the hospital's gender identity services were available only to individuals who had obtained a court-ordered gender recognition certificate. Reyes had not yet begun the legal recognition process, which at the time required medical and psychological evaluation — the very evaluation she was seeking access to through the healthcare system.

== Case ==

Reyes brought a case before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 2013, arguing that the requirement to obtain a court-ordered gender recognition certificate as a precondition for accessing gender-affirming healthcare constituted {{w|discrimination}} on the basis of {{w|gender identity}}. Reyes's legal team argued that the circular dependency between healthcare access and legal recognition created an impossible barrier: she could not obtain a diagnosis without accessing healthcare, and she could not access healthcare without a diagnosis.

The Ministry of Health defended the policy on the grounds that the linkage between healthcare access and legal recognition ensured that medical resources were allocated to individuals with a confirmed medical need. The Ministry argued that the gender recognition process provided the necessary clinical assessment and that decoupling healthcare from legal recognition would compromise the integrity of both systems.

== Ruling ==

The Tribunal ruled in Reyes's favour in 2014, finding that the linkage between healthcare access and the medicalised legal recognition process created a discriminatory barrier. The Tribunal held that the requirement to obtain a court-ordered gender recognition certificate as a precondition for healthcare access was inconsistent with the [[Twelve Guarantees]]' anti-discrimination provisions, as it imposed a burden on transgender individuals that was not imposed on cisgender individuals seeking comparable healthcare services.

The ruling did not immediately change the law, as the Tribunal's jurisdiction was limited to ordering remedies in individual cases. However, the ruling established that the existing gender recognition framework was inconsistent with the Twelve Guarantees and signalled to the legislature that reform was necessary.

== Significance ==

The Reyes ruling became the primary legal basis for the Gender Recognition Act of 2017, which adopted a {{w|self-determination}} model for legal gender change and decoupled legal recognition from medical requirements. Under the Act, individuals may change their legal gender by {{w|statutory declaration}} without requiring medical diagnosis, psychological evaluation, or surgical intervention. The Act also provides for {{w|non-binary}} legal recognition.

Following the 2017 Act, the Ministry of Health issued a revised directive removing the psychiatric diagnosis requirement for HRT access, replacing it with an {{w|informed consent}} model in which a general practitioner or endocrinologist could prescribe hormones following a clinical assessment of the patient's understanding of the effects and risks.

Reyes became a prominent advocate for {{w|transgender rights}} in Balboa following the ruling. She was appointed to the advisory board of the Office of LGBT Affairs in 2023 and was a leading voice in the campaign for the [[Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections]] (2019), which added gender identity to the protected categories under the [[Twelve Guarantees]].

